# Documentation and Comments Language

## Rule

**All code-level content must be in English**, regardless of developer locale:

- Source code comments (`//`, `/* */`, `/** */`, `#`)
- Rust doc-comments (`///`, `//!`)
- TypeScript JSDoc / TSDoc blocks
- Commit messages
- PR titles and descriptions
- Branch names
- README and all Markdown docs in the repo

## App UI is Multilingual

The application itself supports multiple languages. Localisation strings live in the UI layer and are **not** subject to this rule. User-facing text should use i18n keys, not hardcoded strings.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — hardcoded UI string
<button>Save context</button>

// ✅ GOOD — i18n key
<button>{t('context.save')}</button>
```

## Code Comments

```rust
// ❌ BAD
// Aplica los contextos activos al archivo hosts
fn apply_to_hosts(...) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
// Applies all enabled contexts to the system hosts file.
// Replaces the BYPASS-managed block; leaves user-owned entries untouched.
fn apply_to_hosts(...) { ... }
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
// Carga los contextos desde el backend
async function load() { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
// Fetches all contexts from the Rust backend and updates the store.
async function load() { ... }
```

## Doc-comments

```rust
/// Builds the managed hosts block from the list of enabled contexts.
///
/// Returns an empty string if no contexts are enabled.
fn build_managed_block(contexts: &[Context]) -> String { ... }
```

## Commit message examples

```
feat: apply hosts on context content update
fix: revert persisted state when hosts write fails
docs: fix README CI workflow filename reference
```
